# TLS front for the four public names. Certificates are issued automatically by
# Let's Encrypt once the DNS records exist; Caddy keeps retrying until then.
#
# The four hostnames and the ACME contact come from the environment, set in
# `infra/selfhost/.env` and passed through by the compose file:
#
#   OA_APP_HOST        the dashboard          app.analytics.example
#   OA_API_HOST        the control plane      api.analytics.example
#   OA_COLLECTOR_HOST  ingest, and oa.js      c.analytics.example
#   OA_REALTIME_HOST   the SSE stream         rt.analytics.example
#
# All four must resolve to this host before the first start, or issuance fails
# and Caddy serves nothing on 443.

{
	email {$OA_ACME_EMAIL}
}

# ===========================================================================
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING BELOW.
#
# `client_identity_hygiene` is a SECURITY CONTROL, not tidiness. Import it into
# every route that reaches the collector, the api or the realtime stream.
#
# The collector reads the visitor's address from a small list of headers a
# fronting platform is expected to set from the connection itself — `X-Real-IP`,
# `CF-Connecting-IP`, `Fly-Client-IP`, `True-Client-IP` — plus country and city
# headers of the same kind (`apps/collector/src/client-identity.ts`). On a
# platform like Vercel or Cloudflare that is safe, because the platform
# overwrites those headers on every request and a client cannot forge one.
#
# BEHIND YOUR OWN PROXY, NOTHING OVERWRITES THEM UNLESS YOU DO. A visitor who
# simply sends `CF-Connecting-IP: 203.0.113.10` would then choose:
#
#   * their own rate-limit bucket — send a fresh address per request and the
#     per-IP limiter never fires, which is the whole of the ingest flood
#     defence;
#   * their own anonymous visitor identity, since the daily-rotating hash is
#     derived from that address — so one client can appear as any number of
#     distinct visitors, or impersonate another;
#   * their own country and city in your analytics, by sending the geo headers
#     directly.
#
# The two directives that prevent all three: assert `X-Real-IP` from the actual
# connection, and DELETE every other header in that list so nothing a client
# sent can survive. Caddy already replaces `X-Forwarded-For` for untrusted
# clients by default, and the collector reads its RIGHTMOST entry — the address
# the closest trusted proxy observed — so a prepended entry buys nothing.
#
# If you put another proxy, CDN or load balancer IN FRONT of this one, this
# snippet becomes wrong as written: it would then assert the address of that
# proxy for every visitor. Configure the outer layer to set `X-Real-IP` from the
# connection, keep the deletions here, and drop the `header_up X-Real-IP` line.
# ===========================================================================
(client_identity_hygiene) {
	header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	header_up -Fly-Client-IP
	header_up -CF-Connecting-IP
	header_up -True-Client-IP
	header_up -X-Vercel-IP-Country
	header_up -X-Vercel-IP-City
	header_up -Fly-Client-Country
	header_up -CF-IPCountry
	header_up -CF-IPCity
}

# --- the dashboard ---------------------------------------------------------
# A Next.js app. It calls the api from the visitor's browser, so the api host
# below must appear in the api's AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS or every one of those
# calls dies in CORS.
{$OA_APP_HOST} {
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}

# --- the control plane -----------------------------------------------------
{$OA_API_HOST} {
	reverse_proxy api:8082 {
		import client_identity_hygiene
	}
}

# --- ingest, and the tracker itself ----------------------------------------
# `oa.js` is what a customer pastes into their page, so it is served from the
# host they already point at — the shape the install snippet uses, and one fewer
# name to own a certificate for.
#
# Caddy serves it, not the collector: a static file has no business travelling
# through a Node request pipeline with its logging, metrics and rate limiting,
# and Caddy already does conditional requests and compression correctly. The
# file is written into the shared `tracker` volume by the one-shot
# `tracker-build` container.
{$OA_COLLECTOR_HOST} {
	handle /oa.js {
		root * /srv/tracker

		# The filename is stable and never versioned. A versioned name would be
		# cacheable forever but would make every fix require every site owner to
		# edit their snippet. So: one hour of freshness, then an ETag
		# revalidation that costs a 304. That hour is also the ceiling on how
		# long a broken tracker survives after you have fixed it.
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600"

		# Not needed to LOAD the script — a classic `<script src>` is not a CORS
		# request — but it is what lets a site owner add `crossorigin` and get
		# real messages in `window.onerror` instead of "Script error.". The file
		# is public by definition; there is nothing to withhold.
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"

		# Served precompressed rather than compressed per request: the build
		# enforces a GZIPPED size budget, and the `tracker-build` container wrote
		# `oa.js.gz` beside `oa.js`, so the bytes on the wire are the bytes the
		# budget measured — at no CPU cost per hit. Caddy falls back to the plain
		# file for a client that does not accept gzip.
		file_server {
			precompressed gzip
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy collector:8083 {
			import client_identity_hygiene
		}
	}
}

# --- the realtime stream ---------------------------------------------------
# `flush_interval -1` disables response buffering so every event leaves the
# proxy the moment the service writes it. Without it Caddy may hold small chunks
# and a "realtime" stream arrives in bursts.
{$OA_REALTIME_HOST} {
	reverse_proxy realtime:8084 {
		import client_identity_hygiene
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
